{
"Ambiguity is hard to pin down.": "f8c1cec50e5c",
"An argument that is obviously bad does little harm, because nobody is taken in by it. The dangerous ones are those that pass for good. Aristotle opened the first book ever written on the subject with exactly this observation: That some reasonings are genuine, while others seem to be so but are not, is evident. It happens, he added, through a certain likeness between the genuine and the sham, in the way that some people are healthy and others only made up to look it. An argument of the second kind is called a fallacy. The word does not mean a false belief, though it is often used that way. It names a fault in the route, and the destination may be true or false.": "a78812c50728",
"Buddhist logicians later did something the Western tradition did not manage. They derived their fallacies from a single principle. Dignāga held that a good reason must satisfy three conditions (trairūpya): it must be a property of the subject, it must be present in similar cases, and it must be absent from all dissimilar cases. A fallacious reason (hetvābhāsa, 似因) is one that fails a condition, and so there are three kinds: the unestablished, the inconclusive, and the contradictory. Hamblins complaint that the Western list has no principle behind it does not apply here. The price is narrowness: the scheme covers faults of inference and has nothing to say about a straw man (Dharma and the West, D14).": "7958a6e633e7",
"Fallacies of ambiguity turn on language.": "0b2b708b06c6",
"Fallacies of ambiguity turn on language. In equivocation a word is used in two senses, and the argument is valid only if the sense stays fixed. Nothing is better than good health; a cold sandwich is better than nothing; so a cold sandwich is better than good health. The joke is harmless, but the same mechanism does real damage when the shifting word is natural, theory, or freedom.": "eb0cc75f2197",
"Fallacies of presumption smuggle in what they ought to establish.": "39886e2743eb",
"Fallacies of presumption smuggle in what they ought to establish. Begging the question assumes the conclusion in the premises, usually in different words. A false dilemma offers two options as though there were no others. A slippery slope asserts, without evidence for each step, that one small move will end in disaster. A hasty generalization leaps from a few cases to all of them. Post hoc ergo propter hoc, after this, therefore because of this, infers a cause from a mere sequence. And the straw man misrepresents an opponents view in a weaker form, knocks that down, and claims the victory over the original.": "832d5675dcc3",
"Fallacies of relevance offer something that moves us in place of something that bears on the question at issue. The ad hominem reply attacks the speaker and leaves the argument standing. One common version, tu quoque (you too), tries to discredit advice by pointing out that the adviser does not follow it, as though a doctor who smokes must be wrong about smoking. The appeal to popularity treats the number of believers as evidence. The appeal to authority cites someone eminent on a matter outside his competence: an actor on medicine, a physicist on diet. Appeals to pity or fear work the same way. In every case the premise may well be true, and the conclusion is no more likely for it.": "9da9934b38cd",
"Fallacies succeed on the strength of what we already want to believe.": "57b1189d4028",
"Here the subject becomes interesting, because almost every item on the list has a respectable twin. Trusting an authority is how any of us knows that the earth is four and a half billion years old; the appeal is fallacious only when the authority is speaking outside his field, or when the experts themselves disagree. A witnesss record of lying is a fact about the person, and it is plainly relevant to whether we should accept his testimony. Some slopes really are slippery, and saying so is reasonable when each step is supported. Even a small sample can justify a conclusion if the cases are known to be alike.": "9674b2dc7e7e",
"If fallacies are so well cataloged, why do they still succeed? Francis Bacon gave the classic answer in 1620. The human understanding, when any proposition has been once laid down, he wrote, forces everything else to add fresh support and confirmation, and it neglects or explains away the instances on the other side. Psychologists now call this confirmation bias. Fallacies succeed for the most part on the strength of what we already want to believe. That explains the least comfortable fact about them: they are easy to spot in an opponent and almost invisible in a friend.": "b4edb317ce84",
"It neglects or explains away the instances on the other side.": "85b4f416b565",
"Nobody is taken in by an argument that is obviously bad.": "8c0d8016bf26",
"So a fallacy cannot be identified by its outward shape. One has to ask what work the premise is supposed to do and whether it does it. It follows that there is a fallacy about fallacies: to show that an argument is bad is not to show that its conclusion is false. A true claim can be defended badly, and often is.": "ada63894c980",
"That some reasonings are genuine, while others seem to be so but are not, is evident. This happens with arguments, as also elsewhere, through a certain likeness between the genuine and the sham.": "d9b452296c03",
"The Latin labels are useful, and they are also a temptation. To answer an argument by shouting straw man or ad hominem is to stand a label in for a reply, which is itself a failure of relevance. The better practice is slower. Say what the premise was meant to show, say why it does not show it, and then ask whether the argument can be repaired. Often it can. A slippery slope becomes a decent argument once the steps are supported. A hasty generalization becomes a hypothesis worth testing. Used this way, the catalog is less a list of crimes than a set of diagnoses, and its first patient should be oneself.": "c5ea77dfd1f1",
"The dangerous arguments are those that pass for good.": "de28acc0594c",
"The discourse to the Kālāmas (Aṅguttara Nikāya 3.65) reads in places like a list of fallacies of relevance. The Buddha tells a puzzled town not to settle a question by oral tradition, by lineage, by hearsay, by the authority of scripture, by the speakers apparent competence, or by the thought this ascetic is our teacher. The list also includes by logic and by inference, which shows that it is not a charter for free thinking, as it is often taken to be. Its point is that none of these sources is sufficient by itself. The test he gives is practical: whether a thing, when taken up, leads to harm or to welfare.": "2d5c64676a4b",
"The first is, to allege the opinions of men, whose parts, learning, eminency, power, or some other cause has gained a name, and settled their reputation in the common esteem with some kind of authority.": "a0c23bd9dcfc",
"The last lesson dealt with formal fallacies, such as affirming the consequent, where the fault lies in the pattern and can be seen with letters in place of words. Most of the fallacies met in daily life are informal. Their form may be unobjectionable. The trouble lies in what the premises say: they are beside the point, or they quietly assume too much, or a word changes its meaning halfway through. Those three troubles give the traditional three families.": "5f62c52dd68d",
"The most famous case in Chinese philosophy belongs here. Gongsun Long argued in the third century BCE that a white horse is not a horse (白馬非馬). The claim is absurd if 非 means is not a kind of and quite true if it means is not identical with, since the phrase white horse picks out color as well as shape. Whether Gongsun Long was committing an equivocation or exposing one is still debated, and that is some measure of how hard ambiguity is to pin down.": "bfb3afa59246",
"The premise does not bear on the question at issue.": "cebf9f4618a0",
"The straw man misrepresents an opponents view in a weaker form.": "ac13bec39818",
